            <div xml:id="d190">

                <pb facs="0348" n="236" xml:id="pg236"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7030: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-13T14:00:00+08:00">June 13, 1935—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 13—7:03 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>152. 1. Reliably reported that Central Political Council this morning passed
                    resolution to following effect:</p>
                <p>Ho Ying-chin should inform Japanese military authorities in writing: (1) that the
                    Chinese have accepted and are complying with all Japanese demands presented
                    prior to June 11th and (2) that if the Japanese Army has further demands they
                    should be presented to the Chinese Government at Nanking through usual
                    diplomatic channels.</p>
                <p>2. Ho Ying-chin is en route Nanking by plane and it is now generally believed
                    here that Japanese troops will take no direct action in Tientsin-Peiping area
                    until tomorrow or next day after he has had opportunity to confer with his
                    Government.</p>
                <p>3. Ariyoshi is calling upon Wang Ching-wei late today.</p>
                <p>4. Despatched to the Department and Peiping.</p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Atcheson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
